    summary: "Memorial Sloan Kettering's About Herbs entry finds the evidence insufficient to assess calcium plus vitamin D supplementation for people on androgen deprivation therapy, aromatase inhibitors or with chemotherapy-induced menopause, and advises discussing it with the oncology team because calcium interacts with several medicines. Supplementation did not prevent bone density loss in breast cancer patients in the studies MSK cites. Dietary and supplemental calcium are associated with lower colorectal cancer risk, while high calcium intake is associated with prostate cancer; results for other cancers are conflicting. MSK lists the risks of supplements: kidney stones with calcium plus vitamin D, possible increased cardiovascular risk especially in chronic kidney disease, ischaemic stroke with more than 1 g a day, and milk-alkali syndrome, including a life-threatening case in a cancer survivor taking calcium antacids. Interactions include possible hypercalcaemia with tamoxifen, reduced absorption of quinolone and tetracycline antibiotics and of levothyroxine, and competition with iron, zinc and magnesium. Calcium from food is preferred where possible; bone protection on aromatase inhibitors or androgen deprivation is managed with bone density scans and, where needed, prescribed drugs such as bisphosphonates or denosumab.",
